Write on the projector with a screenless tablet

Purpose: lecture with a screenless pen tablet (Windows Ink). The tablet
is mapped to the projector monitor by Windows or the driver; pen input
on the audience window writes onto the page shown there, 1:1.

Behaviour:
- Stylus only; mouse and touch on the projector are ignored.
- Strokes start on the page only, the letterbox is a dead zone; a
  started stroke may leave the page.
- No input while blanked or frozen: the audience would not see it.
  With Lock Page, ink goes onto the locked page.
- A hover ring marks the pen on the projector and the main canvas, the
  only cue a screenless tablet gives. The system cursor is hidden on
  the projector.
- The zoom box is not involved; it stays for pen input on the laptop.
- Tablet (pad) buttons work while the audience window has focus:
  it gets its own pad controller for the main window's pad actions.

Approach:
- Engine: Tablet (crates/rnote-engine/src/tablet.rs) holds hover and
  the dead-zone filter; engine/tablet.rs maps the audience window via
  the inverse audience camera and feeds the pens directly (no box
  drag). The audience window is no longer a passive widget: it gets
  the main canvas' input pipeline through InputSurface::Audience.
- Speed: projector and zoom panel drew every stroke immediate per
  frame, PDF slides rasterized from SVG each time. Cost grew with the
  ink and starved the main window (frames every ~180 ms while writing
  on the projector). store/raster_cache.rs keeps per-stroke rasters
  per view, valid while the store's Arc is unchanged: the store edits
  strokes copy-on-write, so a held Arc never changes in place. Strokes
  being drawn (unique Arc) stay immediate. Both windows now ~60 fps.

Constraints: builds on dual-screen. A very long stroke still slows the
main window while drawn; accepted. No file format change.
